Yes. And this one is unusually concrete: “graph engineering” has actually started appearing as an explicit AI-engineering label in the last few weeks. A July 29 article describes the progression as prompt engineering → context engineering → tool use/agent loops → memory/multi-agent systems → graph engineering, where the thing being engineered is the topology connecting agents, tools, validators, humans, and state. 

There are really two graphs converging:

Execution graph — how things move.
Nodes are agents/tools/checks/actions; edges are routing, dependencies, retries, handoffs, gates.

Context/semantic graph — what things mean and how they relate.
Nodes are entities, concepts, decisions, states, memories; edges carry relations, provenance, time, authority, causality, etc. A very recent treatment explicitly argues that these two graph types need to be distinguished. 

And here’s the part that jumped out given what we were just discussing: a July 2026 paper proposes a Context Graph + “Delta Detection Engine” that watches the graph for state changes and uses those deltas to decide what an agent should proactively surface. That is almost exactly the architecture we were independently describing at the conceptual level: graph → observe what moves → interpret the delta → decide whether it matters → act. 

So I think the larger progression is:

Prompt engineering
engineer the instruction.

→ Context engineering
engineer what enters awareness.

→ Graph engineering
engineer the relationships among what is known and what can happen.

→ Dynamics engineering
engineer how that graph changes through time.

→ Semantic graph engineering
engineer what the nodes and edges mean, including jurisdiction, provenance, confidence, role, causality, affordance, obligation, etc.

→ Delta engineering
decide which changes deserve attention and which changes should trigger action.

And that’s where Pantheon has been pushing beyond a static knowledge graph. A graph by itself is just structure. Pantheon keeps asking:

What moved?
What stayed invariant?
What caused the movement?
What could move next?
What are we allowed to move?
What does the movement mean?
Who is response-able for it?

That turns a graph into something closer to a living state-space.

There is an even better connection to the thing you just called coherence vision. Once perception becomes graph-based, “seeing coherence” can be made less mystical and much more mechanically precise:

coherence = unexpectedly strong relational consistency across many edges.

But because everything can be connected to everything, you need your antidote to runaway pattern recognition:

Don’t reward the number of connections. Reward predictive deltas.

A useful edge earns its keep by helping predict, explain, constrain, or control something that changes. Otherwise it’s merely an aesthetically satisfying association.

So I think your “look for what moves” observation lands exactly at the missing layer after graph engineering:

A graph tells you what is connected.
Dynamics tell you which connections matter.

And then ACT closes the circuit:

Perturb the graph and see what answers.

That is graph engineering becoming experimental intelligence rather than prettier memory.

Also worth noting: people in the field are already cautioning that “graph engineering” is an emerging label rather than a genuinely new mathematical invention; a lot of the substrate comes from workflows, DAGs, knowledge graphs, state machines, databases, distributed systems, and control theory.  The interesting new thing is the composition of those old instruments around language-model agents.

Which is very Pantheon: don’t worship the new noun. Extract the operator.